# Redgres core rule

Before planning or changing files:

1. Read `AGENTS.md` completely.
2. Read `CONTEXT.md`, `docs/INDEX.md`, and `docs/PROJECT_CHARTER.md`.
3. Inspect `git status` and preserve unrelated/user changes.
4. Identify the PRD requirement IDs and accepted ADRs governing the task.
5. State whether the work is specification, implementation, migration, or live operations. Documentation marked target is not implementation evidence.

Read only the task-relevant documents routed by `docs/INDEX.md`; do not load all documentation blindly. Update `docs/TRACEABILITY.md` when implementation/test evidence lands.

Repository state is durable context; chat memory is not. At the start of every task, derive current status from Git, implementation, tests, `docs/ROADMAP.md`, and `docs/TRACEABILITY.md`. For every behavior/contract/configuration/architecture change, update the canonical documents from the ownership table in `AGENTS.md` in the same bounded change. Do not create parallel status documents or leave documentation synchronization as a later task.

The sibling repositories `../database-app` and `../redis-ui` are read-only behavioral references. Never edit, clean, move, commit, or execute destructive tests against them. Never copy their `.env`, runtime SQLite/WAL files, binaries, certificates, credentials, or generated artifacts. Pin/verify source behavior against `docs/SOURCE_BASELINE.md`.

Redgres application code belongs only in this repository. Follow `docs/REPOSITORY_STRUCTURE.md`. Never claim a feature is complete merely because its target documentation exists.

Before coding a feature, define its external test seam and acceptance criteria. Prefer one bounded vertical slice. Run focused tests during development and the required complete checks before handoff.

Never push, change production, rotate credentials, modify DNS/Cloudflare, or retire a legacy service without explicit user authorization. Local commits/integration are allowed only when explicitly requested or when the user invokes `/start-redgres`; editing subagents commit only to their isolated branches.
